ance Statistics (TPFS) provides information on transportation-related revenue and expenditures for all levels of government, including federal, state, and local, and for all modes of transportation.","title":"Aggregate State Transportation Public Finance Tables","programCode":["021:053"],"dataQuality":true,"license":"https://www.usa.gov/government-works","accrualPeriodicity":"R/P1Y","theme":["Research and Statistics"],"language":["en-US"]},{"accessLevel":"public","landingPage":"https://data.bts.gov/d/jtvy-isaj","bureauCode":["021:00"],"issued":"2024-12-09","@type":"dcat:Dataset","modified":"2026-09-29","keyword":["fuel","air carriers","aff"],"contactPoint":{"@type":"vcard:Contact","fn":"BTS Aviation Facts and Figures Program","hasEmail":"mailto:BTSAviationFacts@dot.gov"},"publisher":{"@type":"org:Organization","name":"Bureau of Transportation Statistics"},"identifier":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/jtvy-isaj","description":"Average Fuel Cost by Carrier Group","title":"AFF - P12a - Average Fuel Cost by Carrier Group","programCode":["021:053"],"distribution":[{"@type":"dcat:Distribution","downloadURL":"https://data.bts.gov/api/v3/views/jtvy-isaj/export.csv?accessType=DOWNLOAD","mediaType":"text/csv"},{"mediaType":"application/json","downloadURL":"https://data.bts.gov/api/v3/views/jtvy-isaj/query.json?accessType=DOWNLOAD","describedBy":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/jtvy-isaj/columns.json","describedByType":"application/json","@type":"dcat:Distribution"},{"mediaType":"application/xml","downloadURL":"https://data.bts.gov/api/v3/views/jtvy-isaj/query.xml?accessType=DOWNLOAD","describedBy":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/jtvy-isaj/columns.xml","describedByType":"application/xml","@type":"dcat:Distribution"}],"dataQuality":true,"license":"https://www.usa.gov/government-works","accrualPeriodicity":"R/P1Y","theme":["Aviation"],"language":["en-US"]},{"accessLevel":"public","landingPage":"https://data.bts.gov/d/jyc5-9vwx","bureauCode":["021:00"],"issued":"2019-05-05","@type":"dcat:Dataset","modified":"2026-09-29","keyword":["software","work zone traffic control","transportation improvement projects","traffic delays","intelligent transportation systems","highway operations","highway corridors","decision support systems","strategic planning","strategic highway research program 2","urban renewal","shrp2 project r11","road networks","programming (planning)"],"contactPoint":{"@type":"vcard:Contact","fn":"National Transportation Library Data Curator","hasEmail":"mailto:ntldatacurator@dot.gov"},"publisher":{"@type":"org:Organization","name":"Bureau of Transportation Statistics"},"identifier":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/jyc5-9vwx","description":"As our highway infrastructure continues to age, there is the imperative need to renew the entire network while keeping it operational. Moreover, many highway corridors and regional networks are becoming ever more congested. As a result there is an increasing need to (1) examine various construction alternatives within those corridors and networks to determine the potential disruption and/or benefit that alternative renewal activities may have over time, and (2) examine the effect of constructing multiple corridors concurrently or in sequence. Without an evaluation of the various construction alternatives during the program and project development process, negative impacts on the traveling public and the local communities may increase dramatically. The research on Project R11 focused on answering the following related questions: • How should highway reconstruction projects be set in space and time to minimize disruption to the traveling public, businesses, and adjacent communities? • What is the traffic impact on the regional network when multiple corridors are being considered for renewal? • Are there strategies that may minimize impacts on the corridor and/or regional network?  This zip file contains comma separated value (.csv) files of data to support SHRP 2 report S2-R11-RW-1, Strategic approaches at the corridor and network level to minimize disruption from the renewal process. Zip size is 0.46 MB. Files were accessed in Microsoft Excel 2016. Data will be preserved as is. To view the publication see: https://rosap.ntl.bts.gov/view/dot/3612","title":"Strategic Approaches at the Corridor and Network Level to Minimize Disruption from the Renewal Process [supporting datasets]","programCode":["021:000"],"distribution":[{"mediaType":"application/zip","downloadURL":"https://doi.org/10.21949/1503762","description":"As our highway infrastructure continues to age, there is the imperative need to renew the entire network while keeping it operational. Moreover, many highway corridors and regional networks are becoming ever more congested. As a result there is an increasing need to (1) examine various construction alternatives within those corridors and networks to determine the potential disruption and/or benefit that alternative renewal activities may have over time, and (2) examine the effect of constructing multiple corridors concurrently or in sequence. Without an evaluation of the various construction alternatives during the program and project development process, negative impacts on the traveling public and the local communities may increase dramatically. The research on Project R11 focused on answering the following related questions: • How should highway reconstruction projects be set in space and time to minimize disruption to the traveling public, businesses, and adjacent communities? • What is the traffic impact on the regional network when multiple corridors are being considered for renewal? • Are there strategies that may minimize impacts on the corridor and/or regional network?  This zip file contains comma separated value (.csv) files of data to support SHRP 2 report S2-R11-RW-1, Strategic approaches at the corridor and network level to minimize disruption from the renewal process. Zip size is 0.46 MB. Files were accessed in Microsoft Excel 2016. Data will be preserved as is. To view the publication see: https://rosap.ntl.bts.gov/view/dot/3612","@type":"dcat:Distribution","title":"Strategic Approaches at the Corridor and Network Level to Minimize Disruption from the Renewal Process [supporting datasets]"}],"license":"https://www.usa.gov/government-works","dataQuality":true,"language":["en-US"]},{"accessLevel":"public","landingPage":"https://data.bts.gov/d/jyrm-py8x","bureauCode":["021:00"],"issued":"2021-05-10","@type":"dcat:Dataset","modified":"2026-03-03","keyword":["pocket guide to transportation","passenger travel"],"contactPoint":{"@type":"vcard:Contact","fn":"BTS Pocket Guide to Transportation Data Program","hasEmail":"mailto:btspgt@dot.gov"},"publisher":{"@type":"org:Organization","name":"Bureau of Transportation Statistics"},"identifier":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/jyrm-py8x","description":"Moving People' focuses on the personal mobility of the U.S. population. It captures the 'how, where, and why' of human movement, tracking the shift from daily commutes to leisure travel and long-distance tourism.","title":"Moving People","programCode":["021:053"],"dataQuality":true,"license":"http://www.usa.gov/publicdomain/label/1.0/","accrualPeriodicity":"R/P1Y","theme":["Research and Statistics"],"language":["en-US"]},{"accessLevel":"public","landingPage":"https://data.bts.gov/d/k3id-nynu","bureauCode":["021:04"],"issued":"2020-07-16","@type":"dcat:Dataset","modified":"2026-09-29","keyword":["aviation","noise","transportation noise","roadways","railroad"],"contactPoint":{"@type":"vcard:Contact","fn":"BTS National Transportation Atlas Database Program","hasEmail":"mailto:ntad@dot.gov"},"publisher":{"@type":"org:Organization","name":"Bureau of Transportation Statistics"},"identifier":"https://data.bts.gov/api/views/k3id-nynu","description":"This table shows the number and percent of people in the contiguous United States (excludes Alaska and Hawaii) potentially exposed to different levels of noise from combined 